agent_operability:
After capture, the pipeline is almost entirely command-line and scriptable. Agents can drive:
- ffmpeg frame extraction and equirectangular-to-cubemap splitting;
- COLMAP 4.x or pycolmap (PyPI is reachable from cloud containers);
- Brush CLI, OpenSplat or msplat on the Mac;
- splat-transform (npm, MIT) for cleaning, cluster filtering, LOD, SOG/GLB/SPZ export and collision voxels;
- loading in Spark, PlayCanvas or three r186.

Agents can check renders in the repo's existing headless-Chromium audit (SwiftShader WebGL2/WebGPU), though slowly. Cloud containers have no GPU, so training must run on the Mac (owner-supervised, fanless) or on a rented CUDA box. I have not verified whether splat-transform's GPU steps (cluster filter, voxelisation) run in a GPU-less container.

The capture itself cannot be automated. It needs the owner in Japan, on public trails, ideally coordinated with the Yamanashi Film Commission or a licensed guide. Each hero spot also needs manual cleanup: floaters, sky and people.

Agent usage per hero spot is small compared with hand-modelling. The mesh world still needs its full agent-built pipeline, so splats add work rather than replace it.
